rcq("R07-F-08-3", "Inference", "Difficult",
    "The author says that the fans' champions 'claim too much' (paragraph 3) chiefly because they",
    "keep the assumption that stories can be owned and merely change who the owner is.",
    [("underestimate how much an author loses when readers imagine new episodes for her characters.",
      "Paragraph 3 says it is 'much harder to see what she loses' in such cases, so the author thinks the loss is small, not underestimated."),
     ("ignore the historical evidence that retelling was once the ordinary way in which stories lived.",
      "The champions are said to 'turn this history into a counter-theory', so they use the history rather than ignore it."),
     ("deny that an author may object to having her text copied and sold under another person's name.",
      "The passage nowhere says the champions deny this; it grants the objection as part of the author's own distinction, not as a point the champions reject.")],
    "Paragraph 3: 'To say that a story belongs to those who love it is still to speak of belonging, merely moving the title deeds from one party to another.' The excess is keeping the idea of ownership while changing the owner.",
    "Choosing the copying option because it appears in the same paragraph, though it is the author's concession to authors, not a charge against the champions.",
    "The key paraphrases a metaphor ('title deeds'); a distractor attaches a nearby concession to the wrong party.",
    cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)

rcq("R07-F-08-4", "Inference", "Difficult",
    "The irony described in paragraph 4 lends most support to which of the following ideas?",
    "The urge to guard a story's boundaries comes from attachment to the story, not only from an author's interests.",
    [("Fan communities are, taken as a whole, more hostile to newcomers than authors have ever been to readers.",
      "Paragraph 4 says only that newcomers 'can be' treated with a severity few authors would show; an occasional case is not a verdict on fan communities as a whole against every author, and the irony concerns the source of the guarding impulse."),
     ("Fan writing is in the end a kind of trespass, just as the indignant authors first claimed it was.",
      "The irony is that fans reproduce the proprietary attitude, not that their writing is trespass; paragraph 3 has argued that imagining a world takes nothing from an author."),
     ("Authors ought to adopt the rules that fan communities have developed about what counts as canon.",
      "The passage recommends nothing of the kind; paragraph 4 describes fan rules as reproducing 'the very proprietary attitude it set out to resist'.")],
    "Paragraph 4 ends: 'as though the impulse to fix a story and guard its boundaries were not peculiar to authors but a temptation that comes with loving any story at all.' The irony supports locating the guarding impulse in love of a story rather than in authorship.",
    "Overgeneralising the remark about severity to newcomers into a blanket comparison of fans and authors.",
    "Two options pick up vivid details (severity, trespass); the key needs the point of the irony as a whole.",
    cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)

